WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.567 --> 00:00:01.590
(audience cheering)
- [Kelly] Looking stunning!

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:03.630
- Thank you, you too!
- Looking very rock and roll.

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:05.831
- I love this, that is so cool.

00:00:05.831 --> 00:00:06.840
- Thank you! Okay, I love this too,

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.430
but this is my favorite.
- [Olivia] Wow!

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:09.660
- I got it from Josh Brolin,
it was from The Goonies!

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.070
- No, what? Casual.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:12.150
- [Kelly] It's from The Goonies!

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:13.770
- Oh my gosh, cool!

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:14.603
- I told him, I was like,

00:00:14.603 --> 00:00:15.480
I'm gonna make it a necklace,

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.280
and I think he probably thinks
I'm a stage five clinger now

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.320
but I just loved The Goonies as a kid.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:22.674
Anyway, welcome back to LA!
- Thank you, thank you.

00:00:22.674 --> 00:00:23.507
(audience applauds)
I love being here.

00:00:23.507 --> 00:00:25.290
- You spend your time between
here and London, right?

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:28.016
- Yes, been going back
and forth and it's good.

00:00:28.016 --> 00:00:28.849
- How is that with kids?

00:00:28.849 --> 00:00:30.900
- You know what? They're
great, they have each other.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.570
I have a eight-year-old
and an almost six-year-old,

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:35.158
which I know is
- Oh, it's similar!

00:00:35.158 --> 00:00:35.991
- Similar to you.
- Yeah, exactly.

00:00:35.991 --> 00:00:37.872
- And they're best friends and so

00:00:37.872 --> 00:00:39.090
- Mine are too.
- They have been bouncing

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:40.920
around the world together
their whole lives

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.600
and they're rolling with it.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:43.433
- What's important about that,

00:00:43.433 --> 00:00:44.266
and I don't know if you say this,

00:00:44.266 --> 00:00:45.300
is I always tell them like,

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:46.860
Look, you're each other's buddy,

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.150
you're each others' constant.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:49.980
You might be with daddy or
you might be with mommy,

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.450
but you're always gonna be together.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:52.620
So look out for each other.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:53.550
- I'm so grateful they
have each other like that.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:54.930
- Yeah and my kids have been traveling

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:57.060
since they were young too,
and I think it's helpful.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:57.893
They love it.
- Oh, yeah!

00:00:57.893 --> 00:00:59.070
- They don't think anything of it.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:00.030
- Yeah, it's cool!

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:01.680
I think it makes them really adaptable

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.810
and they meet friends really easily now

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.160
and I think it's been good for them.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.260
- I think so too. Yeah, it
makes them very adaptable.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:09.593
- Yeah, thank God.
- We're both single parents.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:15.660
It can be taxing I feel like,
but what is your take on it,

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:17.370
as far as single
parenting? I'm interested.

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:18.960
- You know, it's tough.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:23.402
I think reshaping a family is tricky

00:01:23.402 --> 00:01:25.170
and the one benefit is, it's allowed for

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.540
some really deep
conversations with my kids,

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.520
like about emotions and about happiness

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.270
and about what family means and love.

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:36.000
It's actually allowed
me to get to know them

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.845
in a different way and
my priority is them.

00:01:39.845 --> 00:01:40.678
- Amen, yeah.
- That's what it is.

00:01:40.678 --> 00:01:44.610
As long as they're happy
and they're healthy then...

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.470
My ex and I, we agree on that,

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:48.213
they are everything to us.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:54.060
I think that there's so
many families who are

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:55.663
blended in different shapes.

00:01:55.663 --> 00:01:56.577
- Oh my god, I'm from one!

00:01:56.577 --> 00:01:58.320
And I have one right now, it's hard.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.970
- [Olivia] It's hard but I just-

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:02.130
- It's doable.
- It's doable.

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:04.620
If you can surround
them with so much love,

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.590
then it's okay, but it's tricky

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:09.900
cause we're not doing it in private.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.235
- That's what I was gonna lead to, I know.

00:02:12.235 --> 00:02:14.490
My thing is, it's hard enough in general,

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:15.390
and I've been through divorce

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:17.553
with my family as a kid, a couple times.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.660
It's hard enough, but then
you do it in the public

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:25.380
and it's like, Okay, so
everyone thinks they know

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:28.083
the entire story. That's super fun.

00:02:28.083 --> 00:02:30.180
(both chuckle)

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.040
Cause you're trying to guard,

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:33.270
so you can't talk about it too much,

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:35.010
especially with children.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:37.470
Any time you have any kind
of separation with children,

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:42.060
it's so hard because you're
trying to help their heart.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.010
That was the hardest part for me.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:45.000
It's already devastating

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:46.620
when you split from
someone you love, right,

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.840
and your family kind of
does this different thing.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:50.580
That's already devastating enough,

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.070
but under a microscope
of everyone's advice

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:55.323
and scrutiny and shame, that's super fun.

00:02:55.323 --> 00:02:56.703
- Yes, and shame.

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:00.930
- You really wanna help control

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:02.876
the narrative for your kids, right?

00:03:02.876 --> 00:03:03.709
You do a good job saying,

00:03:03.709 --> 00:03:05.280
Look, mommy and daddy love you.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.530
You are safe and everything
is gonna be okay.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.150
Family is family.

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:12.150
But when the world is
forming their own narrative

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.340
and when you can't control it

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:17.160
and your very private,
personal, complex breakup

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:20.460
is reduced to tabloid
fodder and headlines...

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.710
When I realized I just had
to stop trying to correct

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:25.890
incorrect narratives, I
just had to realize...

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:28.170
- Oh, you're yelling into the wind.

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:30.183
- Just let it be and focus on them.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.810
In a way, it's like they've
helped me get through it

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:35.190
because I just return to them

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:38.736
and if they're okay, then
I can be strong for them.

00:03:38.736 --> 00:03:39.569
(audience cheering)

00:03:39.569 --> 00:03:40.870
- We're back with Olivia Wilde, y'all!

00:03:42.274 --> 00:03:44.490
Her new film is called
Don't Worry Darling.

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.620
It opens this Friday
and it's a great movie

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:48.870
to see in the theater with a
group of friends, just sayin'.

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:50.310
You gotta check it out.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:52.380
For those who don't know about the film,

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:54.600
could you explain the premise?

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:57.840
- It's about a woman who may
or may not be losing her mind

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.240
and she's starting to question
her reality and she lives

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.600
in this absolute paradise
of 1950s Palm Springs.

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:04.710
Everything is beautiful,

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:07.290
everyone's happy,
everyone's having martinis

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:09.930
and everyone's just partying all the time.

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:12.270
She's deeply in love with her husband,

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:14.460
but she starts to feel like
either she's going crazy

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:18.813
or something is very
wrong with this world. Oh.

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:21.510
- I literally was watching it by myself,

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:23.160
obviously my kids
weren't watching with me,

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:27.120
and I was like, What? Every few minutes.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.040
- It's like a good juicy thriller.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.050
I love reading juicy thrillers

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:35.040
and it is that kind of
satisfying, twisty turny...

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.260
I had such a great team on it.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:40.470
I had an amazing cinematographer,
so it looks incredible.

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:42.870
The music, the production design.

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:46.473
We recreated these 1950s-
- The costumes, the makeup.

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:51.480
- It's a kind of delicious
movie to take in.

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:53.580
The performances, cause
I had this amazing cast,

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:55.290
the performances are all great.

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:56.970
- Florence?
- I know, Florence Pugh.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:58.800
- You were almost gonna play
that role though, right?

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.690
- I was gonna play the
role like early, early on

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:03.360
and then I saw Midsommar with Florence.

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:04.560
Then I met her and I was like,

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.440
that's it, lights out, she's our lead.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.770
She's outstanding. I
mean, she's unbelievable.

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:13.249
We're so lucky to have her as a lead.

00:05:13.249 --> 00:05:14.280
She's the heart of the film,

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:15.990
she's also like an action hero,

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:18.120
as you could see in the end of the movie.

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:19.680
She's incredible.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.330
- Well I've seen her in
another movie, Black Widow.

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:23.040
She yeah, she can do action.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.666
But also, one of the cast is Harry Styles.

00:05:25.666 --> 00:05:26.839
- Yes, he's great.

00:05:26.839 --> 00:05:28.257
- So Harry is, he's this huge entertainer.

00:05:28.257 --> 00:05:29.730
(audience cheers)
(Olivia claps)

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:30.570
Tell me what that's like,

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:32.910
cause having such a huge
entertainer that people know

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:34.770
from a different form of entertainment.

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:36.840
What was that like
directing someone like that?

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:38.670
- I think musicians are always good.

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:40.200
I think there's something about music

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.666
where you guys have to commit-

00:05:41.666 --> 00:05:43.103
- Have you seen From Justin to Kelly?

00:05:43.103 --> 00:05:47.823
(both laughing)
This is not true.

00:05:49.517 --> 00:05:51.949
- The thing about musicians
is like, you guys commit.

00:05:51.949 --> 00:05:53.453
- [Kelly] We do.
- You can't not commit.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:56.220
I used to direct music videos

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:59.340
and I used to be amazed that musicians,

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:01.770
every single take, they'd
go for it, you go for it!

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:03.421
It's like you can't not,

00:06:03.421 --> 00:06:06.060
and I think that's why directing
musicians and dancers, too

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:08.190
and athletes, like they go for it,

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:10.140
and Harry was no different.

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:12.150
It was so professional and so committed

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:14.430
and such a joy and the whole cast.

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:16.650
I had like the coolest ensemble.

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:18.307
Chris Pine, who's so good in it.

00:06:18.307 --> 00:06:20.370
- God, Cr...Whatever, I'm
not gonna say anything

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:22.710
what I was gonna say. I
don't wanna ruin anything.

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:23.970
Wow, just crazy.

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:26.220
I was like, Okay!
- [Olivia] He's so good.

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:27.870
- Everyone is so good!

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:29.094
- And we were going through it-

00:06:29.094 --> 00:06:29.970
- It's nice when chemistry
just happens like that.

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:31.410
Like it happens, you know?

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:33.750
- It was like in the
middle of the pandemic-

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:36.120
- That's fun to work in.
- And we were all in a bubble

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:37.570
and never saw anyone's mouth,

00:06:38.466 --> 00:06:41.970
and it was really amazing
because everyone just bonded.

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:43.770
We knew we wanted it, we were so lucky

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:46.260
to be making art in the midst of what was

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:48.270
a difficult time for everybody.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:51.630
We had each other's backs
and we pulled it off.

00:06:51.630 --> 00:06:53.070
Now we finally get to...

00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:55.050
This is like three years of
my life making this movie

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:56.548
and now it's like, Here world!

00:06:56.548 --> 00:06:58.068
- [Kelly] That's what's so weird about it.

00:06:58.068 --> 00:07:00.330
It takes so long to finally
long come to fruition.

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:02.040
- It takes so long and we
really wanted to get it right.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:03.770
- Job well done.
- Thank you.

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:05.310
- You got it right, it was just so good.

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:06.810
- We're lucky, we get
to come out in theaters

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:09.840
and so many movies don't, and
now it's a director's dream.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:11.520
But I'm aware when audiences go out

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:12.810
and they're gonna spend money on a ticket

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:13.950
and they're gonna get a babysitter

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:14.970
and they're gonna go to the theater,

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:16.170
I want it to be an experience.

00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:17.190
I want it to be a ride.

00:07:17.190 --> 00:07:20.303
I want them to feel like
they had a fun time.

00:07:20.303 --> 00:07:21.973
- I would've enjoyed that.

00:07:21.973 --> 00:07:24.030
I was in my house alone, but
I would've enjoyed hearing...

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:25.440
Cause you get the gasps, right?

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:28.410
You get the like, Oh!
From the whole audience.

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:29.820
That energy is really cool.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:31.959
- That's the best, I love it.

00:07:31.959 --> 00:07:33.263
- It's so good.
- I'm really excited

00:07:33.263 --> 00:07:34.680
people will get to go and
have that experience together.

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:37.620
We're lucky to be able to go
together to theaters again.

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:39.360
This one is a real movie movie

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:41.340
that makes you feel that satisfaction

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:43.500
of the communal theatrical experience.

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:44.670
- But by the end of the film, you're like,

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:47.124
Oh my God, I ate the
entire vessel of popcorn.

00:07:47.124 --> 00:07:48.226
- Yeah, it's a real anxiety popcorn-

00:07:48.226 --> 00:07:50.280
- Cause you're just like,
Oh my God, oh my God.

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:53.310
I wasn't gonna eat pizza
last night and I ordered it.

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:54.630
I ordered it during the film.

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:56.160
I was like, Oh I need something.

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:59.204
I was like, I need something
delicious in my mouth.

00:07:59.204 --> 00:08:00.441
- It's a stress-eater movie for sure.

00:08:00.441 --> 00:08:01.400
- It's stressful but it's great.

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:02.233
(audience applauding)

00:08:02.233 --> 00:08:03.180
A lot has been said recently,

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:04.770
and we don't need to rehash it,

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:07.830
I wouldn't like to, cause I get it,

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:09.390
but how do you keep your composure

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:12.723
when so much of your
private life is made public?

00:08:14.718 --> 00:08:15.780
It's hard to smile.

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:17.913
- It's hard sometimes, and you know,

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:21.240
but at the end of the day, I'm so lucky.

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:23.520
I'm so lucky and there's
people dealing with

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:25.500
so many really hard things.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:27.480
The people of Jackson,
Mississippi don't have water.

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:30.460
There's people everywhere
dealing with real health crises

00:08:32.552 --> 00:08:34.740
and my stuff is out there, but I'm alive

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:37.560
and my kids are alive and
that's what gets me through.

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:40.053
I remind myself of that every single day.

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:42.360
- You have to so you don't get resentful.

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:44.460
- Exactly, and like I said,

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:46.470
once you stop trying to
control the narrative...

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:49.170
I'm not on the Internet,
I'm not on Instagram.

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:51.360
- I don't advise it.
(Olivia laughs)

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:53.280
I literally tell, especially
all the young people

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:54.690
that come in, as a young
musician and all that,

00:08:54.690 --> 00:08:58.669
I'm like, I get that it's for
work, but don't live on it.

00:08:58.669 --> 00:08:59.644
- [Olivia] It's a losing battle.

00:08:59.644 --> 00:09:00.477
- It's no good for anyone.

00:09:00.477 --> 00:09:01.320
You're either gonna
drink your own Kool-Aid

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:02.690
and think you're amazing,

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:04.736
or you're gonna wanna
crawl in a hole and die.

00:09:04.736 --> 00:09:05.690
- (laughs) It's a lose-lose!

00:09:06.533 --> 00:09:08.085
- Neither option is
super great for anyone.

00:09:08.085 --> 00:09:09.810
- Exactly, so I think that really helps.

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:12.570
Focusing on what's real, your
trusted circle of friends,

00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:14.400
what's real, things that make you happy,

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:16.443
people you love, people who love you.

00:09:18.475 --> 00:09:19.410
Just keeping your mind in what's real,

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:20.790
I think that's how I get through it.

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:23.940
Also watching people, my
colleagues, get through it.

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:26.010
I think you're a real
great example of that.

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:28.290
Like, you come out here
and you smile and you laugh

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:31.140
and you give joy and
that's inspiring to me,

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:32.220
and I'm sure a lot of other people

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:34.020
who are going through hard things.

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:36.213
So that's why this is important.

00:09:37.264 --> 00:09:40.860
I think, God, it could be a lot worse

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:43.893
and we're alive and
everything's gonna be okay.

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:47.850
- It's so funny, I told you backstage,

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:50.610
it has not been fun but
it is important, though.

00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:51.750
I think that doing this show

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:53.280
and highlighting those
people that you said,

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:54.660
there are real crises going on,

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:56.760
there are also really
amazing things going on.

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:59.550
It's nice, it's like, look,
the world is not about me,

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:01.620
let's focus on something
else, it helps you.

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:03.390
It helps you get through.
- It's the most healing thing.

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:04.860
- Also, you mentioned friends.

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:07.020
I have no idea how people
go through separations

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:11.130
or any kind of trauma
without a circle of trust.

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:13.631
Those friends, I would not have made it.

00:10:13.631 --> 00:10:14.940
I wouldn't have made it.
(audience applauds)

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:17.430
- No, I wouldn't, I would not be here.

00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:19.470
- You need that support, so
I'm glad that you had that.

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:20.460
That's very, very important.

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:23.040
Are you ever tempted to respond?

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:25.470
At one point, someone
said something about me,

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:28.020
well, many points, but at one point

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:30.533
I was just like, You just
got voted off the island.

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:34.260
Just to be funny, I was
just like, Whatever.

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:35.970
Are you ever tempted?

00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:38.816
I hate when I do it, but
sometimes I'm like... (groans)

00:10:38.816 --> 00:10:40.050
- So tempting! It's so tempting!

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:41.340
And then I think like, Michelle Obama,

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:43.230
they go low, we go high,
they go low, we go high.

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:45.540
I try, but it's hard.

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:49.140
And I think, what am I
modeling for my kids?

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:51.090
How do I want my daughter in high school

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:52.170
to deal with bullies?

00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:54.390
I want her to just keep
her eye on the prize

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:56.340
and keep going and ignore the noise.

00:10:56.340 --> 00:10:59.280
That's what stops me
from getting involved.

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:01.770
It's just a losing battle,
once you get sucked into it,

00:11:01.770 --> 00:11:03.360
it eats you up.
- [Kelly] It's perpetual.

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:04.740
- But yeah, of course it's tempting,

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:05.940
and then that's why you have your friends

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:09.000
and your pillow to scream
into and everything else.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:11.466
- I do this, I send
what I really wanna say

00:11:11.466 --> 00:11:12.299
- [Olivia] To a friend?
- To a friend.

00:11:12.299 --> 00:11:14.569
- That's genius! Can
I just send it to you?

00:11:14.569 --> 00:11:17.520
- Yes, I'm like, God I
wish I could post this!

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:20.370
I'm like, This would be
amazing, it would feel so good!

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:22.290
You know when people are like,
Will it make you feel good?

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:24.762
I'm like, damn right! Yes!
- [Olivia] Yes, it will!

00:11:24.762 --> 00:11:25.749
It will feel great.

00:11:25.749 --> 00:11:26.787
- It actually would make me feel amazing!

00:11:28.233 --> 00:11:31.350
It's hard, it's a hard
thing, but you're right.

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:33.307
First world problems
at the end of the day.

00:11:33.307 --> 00:11:35.970
I wanted to talk about this too because,

00:11:35.970 --> 00:11:39.270
do you feel that the press
surrounding this movie

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:40.830
is a bit of a double standard?

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:42.840
Because I kept finding myself going,

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:45.420
Oh, this has happened like, a lot.

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:46.653
- I don't disagree.

00:11:48.868 --> 00:11:50.250
I think that you find that everywhere.

00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:51.990
Every woman can kind of relate to that.

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:55.680
I think also, this business,

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:57.420
I was aware when I got into it,

00:11:57.420 --> 00:11:59.280
it's male-dominated, it
has been for a long time.

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:01.710
Being a female director,
there's not that many of us.

00:12:01.710 --> 00:12:04.170
Not for lack of talent, just
for lack of opportunity.

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:05.520
There's a lot of great young filmmakers

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:08.550
out there that are women,
so I'm prepared for that.

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:10.890
I think that when it hurts me is sometimes

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:12.060
when it's coming from other women

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:13.650
and I'm like, Can we just give each other

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:17.220
the benefit of the doubt and
just have each other's backs?

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:18.370
Wouldn't that be great?

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:23.070
The coverage always seems to be different.

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:25.620
Am I envious of my male
colleagues in the way that

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:27.420
they seem to be able to live their lives

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:29.700
without as much judgment?
Yeah, I think about it.

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:32.171
I'm like, That must be
nice, to be that guy.

00:12:32.171 --> 00:12:35.460
Everyone's just like,
applauding every move he makes.

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:39.515
But in the end, I'm just
so lucky to have this job,

00:12:39.515 --> 00:12:41.970
I'm so grateful for it.

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:45.180
The coverage, again, it's just noise.

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:48.300
If I get stuck up on
it, I'll get distracted,

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:51.390
so I just blast through.

00:12:51.390 --> 00:12:55.530
- My wish and prayer, cause it was a lot,

00:12:55.530 --> 00:12:58.521
and I was like, Damn! Reading-

00:12:58.521 --> 00:13:00.660
- [Olivia] It's a soap opera, though.

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:02.953
- It's also just like, why do we care?

00:13:02.953 --> 00:13:05.130
- I know.
- Or I don't, sorry.

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:07.530
It just seems to have
overshadowed it a bit,

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:09.450
which is frustrating as an artist,

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:12.120
to watch a female artist
that has had to work so hard

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:14.010
to just do what I love.

00:13:14.010 --> 00:13:16.560
That's a bit upsetting, for me.

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:18.540
Thank you so much, thank you for coming.

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:19.380
- Thank you, you're the best.

00:13:19.380 --> 00:13:20.663
- Thank you for the film.
The film is so, so, so good!

00:13:21.566 --> 00:13:22.870
(audience applauding and cheering)

00:13:22.870 --> 00:13:25.438
Olivia Wilde, everybody!
- Kelly Clarkson!

00:13:25.438 --> 00:13:29.133
- Don't Worry Darling is in
theaters everywhere this Friday.

